  (:adr/body "# Tracking a fleet, and the four enrichments refused

`cloud-itonami/otent` went from three feeds to eight over 2026-08-27/28,
and from 'where are things' to 'who is behind them'. The collection is the
smaller half of what was learned; the refusals are the rest.

## What is collected

| table | rows | source |
|---|---|---|
| `otent_vessel` | 68k+ | Digitraffic (Finnish AIS) + global AIS filtered to listed hulls |
| `otent_vessel_static` | 11.5k | who those vessels say they are |
| `otent_vessel_risk` | 23,173 | OFAC, EU, UK, Swiss, Canadian, Ukrainian lists + MOU detentions |
| `otent_ownership_link` | 3,473 | which organization controls which hull |
| `otent_org_identity` | 1,449 | what identifies those organizations |
| `otent_fire` | 29k+ | NASA FIRMS, global |

`otent sanctions` joins them: 1,375 vessels in coverage, 897 checked, 101
on a list, 45 shadow fleet, 66 sanctioned, 49 with a named controlling
organization.

`bin/collector.cljs` is the only resident process -- a WebSocket to
AISStream, global, filtered to a watchlist built from the risk table.
Measured: 71 messages/second and 5,710 distinct vessels in 90 seconds, a
dedup ratio of 1.12. Keeping everything would be ~4.3 M rows/day against a
read path that already fails at 21,000; filtered it is 124:1.

## Four enrichments refused, each after measuring

**GLEIF by name.** The obvious way to reach a parent company. Sampled 40 of
555 controlling organizations: four exact-name hits, and one agreed on
jurisdiction. GLEIF places `Odyssey Marine Inc.` in Nevada and `Patriot
Inc.` in Delaware where the sanctions record says Marshall Islands.
Recording those as identity asserts that a Nevada company owns a sanctioned
tanker, and the error direction is defamatory.

**GLEIF at all, for this population.** LEI on 2 of 555. The shadow fleet
does not hold LEIs. What it holds is the IMO Company Number, on 478. The
measurement inverted the plan rather than confirming it.

**Joining the two IMO registries.** An IMO Ship Number identifies a hull, an
IMO Company Number an owner; different registries, same seven-digit format,
and in FollowTheMoney the same property name on both schemas. One collision
in 2,607 live values: `IMO9036387` is both the Chinese vessel `New Konk` and
the North Korean firm `KOREA YUJONG SHIPPING CO LTD`. One in 2,607 is the
dangerous frequency -- invisible to a hand-written test, defamatory when it
fires.

**Sanctions data on the CC0 wiki plane.** `wiki.kotobase.net` publishes
claims CC0-1.0; OpenSanctions is CC-BY-NC 4.0. Share-alike is survivable --
`hyakka.license/database-copyleft` keeps the source's licence on the claim
-- but non-commercial is not share-alike and there is no CC0 claim carrying
a use restriction. CC0 is irrevocable. `hyakka.license/use-restricted`
refuses rather than carving out, and `hyakka.senpaku-test` fails if such a
source is added.

## What did go to the wiki

The `senpaku` corpus: 143 tanker identity items, 714 claims, each evidence a
verbatim FIELD FRAGMENT (`\\\"name\\\" : \\\"LOOKMAN\\\"`) of one archived payload
addressed by sha256. Not the record containing it -- a value read from the
wrong field is still inside its record and passes a record-level check.

An IMO check digit was added because the test caught real bad data: one
tanker broadcasting nine digits. All 142 others pass, so it rejects a record
rather than a category, and the exclusion is recorded with its reason.

## Personal data

The governor refused all 1,545 ownership rows on `:person-identifier`,
because `owner` is a person marker. It was protecting something real: 49 of
1,545 edges name a natural person as the owner of a vessel. The answer was
to drop those 49, not to rename the field until the rule stopped noticing.

## Coverage bounds, stated rather than implied

Vessel positions were the Finnish receiver network only until the global
collector landed, which is why the shadow-fleet tankers attacked in the
Black Sea and Mediterranean were absent when asked about on 2026-08-27.
Ownership is the multi-jurisdiction sanctions export: worth +31% globally
and **3.2x for this fleet**, because EU and UK designations target the
Baltic specifically. Quoting the global average would have understated it
tenfold -- the wrong measurement, honestly reported.

## Open

- **Corporate hierarchy is not collected.** OFAC records who owns an asset,
  not who owns the owner, and GLEIF does not cover this population.
- **The port state control join has no target.** Paris MOU serves search as
  an application, Tokyo MOU's database path 404s, EMSA THETIS has no open
  API, Equasis requires registration and forbids bulk extraction. Probed
  2026-08-28. The IMO Company Number is recorded and ready; there is
  nothing openly published to join it to.
- **`hyakka.license/use-restricted` guards only the `senpaku` corpus.**
  Extending it repo-wide is a policy decision, and another session was
  building a sanctions corpus in that repo at the time.
")))
